# RP-3.7: Zero Millisecond Garbage Collection and Memory Safety (ARC/RAII)

| Field | Value |
|-------|--------|
| **Paper ID** | `RP-3.7` |
| **NORTHSTAR.oot** | Section 3 Safety — *Zero millisecond garbage collection and memory safety* |

## Abstract

This paper presents the memory management architecture for the openOODA system. The system requires hard real-time performance to maintain the Observe, Orient, Decide, Act (OODA) loop. Traditional tracing garbage collectors cause unpredictable execution pauses. Manual memory management causes use-after-free errors, double-free errors, and memory leaks. To solve these problems, this architecture uses a layered memory model. The model combines scope-based Resource Acquisition Is Initialization (RAII), Automatic Reference Counting (ARC), and region arenas. This approach provides deterministic, zero-millisecond memory reclamation. It ensures memory safety without the cost of a global garbage collector.

## 1. Introduction

The openOODA system needs predictable latency to operate correctly. A fast feedback loop is necessary for artificial intelligence agents and human developers. Memory management directly affects this latency. Most application memory belongs to a specific request, task, or function block. 

A tracing garbage collector searches the entire memory heap to find unused objects. This search causes a "stop-the-world" pause. These pauses break hard real-time budgets. They cause the system to hesitate unpredictably. 

Conversely, manual memory management requires the developer to free memory explicitly. This manual process is dangerous. It frequently causes use-after-free errors, double-free errors, and memory leaks. These errors account for the majority of memory safety vulnerabilities. They give adversaries a mechanism to corrupt system memory.

To provide safe and predictable execution, openOODA implements a deterministic memory architecture. The system uses Scope-based Resource Acquisition Is Initialization (RAII) and Automatic Reference Counting (ARC). The system also uses region arenas for bulk operations. This model provides C-level steady-state performance. It stops memory leaks and prevents dangerous memory corruption.

## 2. Background and Related Work

The design of the openOODA memory model builds upon several existing systems.

**RAII and Ownership.** Modern C++ uses RAII to connect resource lifetime to scope. Constructors allocate resources, and destructors release them. However, C++ requires strict developer discipline to remain memory safe. Rust uses ownership and borrow checking rules. These rules stop data races and use-after-free errors at compile time. However, the Rust language is complex. It also allows memory leaks through reference cycles if developers do not use weak references correctly.

**Automatic Reference Counting.** Swift and Objective-C use ARC to insert retain and release instructions at compile time. ARC avoids stop-the-world pauses. However, strong reference cycles remain a primary problem. The developer must manually specify weak or unowned references to break these cycles. Python uses classic reference counting and a cycle collector. Python does not meet the requirement for zero-millisecond pauses.

**Arenas and Regions.** Game engines and request handlers frequently use region or arena allocators. These allocators free a full generation of memory in a single, constant-time operation. This aligns with the request and frame lifetimes in openOODA. Academic systems like MLKit use compile-time region inference to place objects in memory automatically.

The openOODA system selects the most predictable elements from these systems. It rejects tracing garbage collection. It defers complex borrow checking. It combines RAII, ARC, and arenas to provide safe, zero-pause memory management.

## 3. Architecture and Methodology

The openOODA memory architecture uses a layered model to manage different data lifetimes.

### 3.1 The Layered Memory Model

The first layer is Scope RAII. This is the default path. When execution leaves a block of code, the system automatically drops local variables. This operation takes zero milliseconds. It is fully deterministic. 

The second layer is ARC. The system uses ARC for shared values. These values include strings, lists, and complex data graphs. When the system copies a reference to an object, it increments the reference count. When a reference goes out of scope, the system decrements the count. The system frees the object immediately when the count reaches zero.

The third layer uses region arenas. The system uses arenas for requests, compile units, and execution frames. An arena allocates memory quickly. When the request finishes, the system resets the entire arena at once. This bulk deallocation is extremely efficient.

The fourth layer is optional temporal memory. The system routes temporal objects to an Event Log Arena. This arena preserves historical states.

### 3.2 Threat Mitigation

This architecture mitigates several critical threats. The system prevents use-after-free errors. An object lifetime ends only when its reference count is zero or when its unique scope drops. The system prevents double-free errors because a single owner makes the free decision. The system prevents latency attacks because there is no global garbage collector to trigger. The system prevents accidental unbounded live sets because scope rules and arenas reclaim bulk memory automatically.

The system does not inherently prevent logical leaks. Containers that grow indefinitely remain live. The system also requires a defined policy to handle retain cycles. 

### 3.3 Conflict Resolution

The memory architecture must resolve conflicts with other system requirements. 

The requirement for instant memory reclamation conflicts with temporal memory rollback. ARC destroys values immediately when the reference count reaches zero. However, temporal memory must keep values reconstructible for a designated period. To resolve this conflict, temporal types are strictly opt-in. The system routes temporal structures to a separate Event Log Arena. The Event Log Arena removes states only after they exceed the time limit. Normal types continue to free instantly. A rollback operation never resurrects a default pointer. 

The requirement for speed conflicts with atomic reference counting. Atomic operations on hot execution paths can slow down the agent loop. To mitigate this, the system uses non-atomic reference counting in single-threaded tasks. The compiler uses escape analysis to remove reference counting operations when they are unnecessary. The system relies on arenas for short-lived bulk data to avoid reference counting overhead entirely.

## 4. Evaluation and Discussion

The proposed architecture achieves the zero-millisecond goal. The system never halts execution to trace live objects. All memory reclamation occurs as a direct result of scope exit or reference count decrement. The latency remains predictable and bounded. 

The system achieves memory safety against use-after-free and double-free errors. The compiler enforces the emission of retain and release instructions. The runtime ensures that memory returns to the system only when the object is provably dead. 

The system maintains a deterministic memory timeline. This determinism is necessary for contracts, fuzzing, and execution replay. Every run of a deterministic program will allocate and free memory in the exact same sequence. 

## 5. Conclusion

The openOODA system requires high performance and high safety. Tracing garbage collectors and manual memory management do not meet these requirements. The layered architecture of Scope RAII, Automatic Reference Counting, and region arenas provides a complete solution. This design gives predictable latency for the OODA feedback loop. It stops memory corruption vulnerabilities. It allows the system to compile code efficiently. This memory model is the foundation for safe, AI-native system operations in openOODA.

